 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples})$ per epoch, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (number of rays), and $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples})$ for storing intermediate rendering states during the forward pass, plus memory for the model parameters [S5, S14, unofficial]. Chunking reduces peak memory usage [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** In the original NeRF implementation, training typically runs for 100,000 iterations with a batch size of 1024 rays [S11, unofficial]. Training on 128x128 images may take 250 epochs [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of training images and the complexity of the scene. Larger batch sizes can improve throughput but may hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable end-to-end, produces high-quality novel views.
-*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence, high memory usage for large scenes, prone to artifacts in sparse-view settings [S1][S17, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required and sufficient multi-view images are available.
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time rendering is required, or when training data is extremely sparse and unstructured without additional priors.
+- **Time Complexity**: $O(N_{iters} \cdot B \cdot K \cdot C)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $B$ is the batch size (number of rays), $K$ is the number of samples per ray, and $C$ is the cost of the neural network forward pass [S5, S14, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: $O(B \cdot K \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the dimensionality of the network activations. VRAM usage is a critical constraint, often mitigated by chunking rays [S5, S14, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance**: Training on 128x128 images for 250 epochs can yield PSNR improvements from ~10.6 to ~18.9 dB [S12]. Training on 199 images (downsampled 4x) with batch size 1024 results in ~7,800 iterations per epoch [S17, unofficial].
+- **Scaling**: Performance scales with the number of training views and the complexity of the scene. Larger batch sizes (e.g., $2^{14}$) can improve throughput but may hinder data loading performance and GPU occupancy, making smaller batches (e.g., $2^{10}$) a practical trade-off [S15, unofficial].
+- **Strengths**: Simple to implement, differentiable, and produces high-quality novel views for dense input sets [S5, S14, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: Slow convergence for large scenes, high memory usage, and prone to artifacts (floaters) in sparse-view settings [S1][S17, unofficial].
+- **When to Use**: Dense multi-view reconstruction with known camera poses [S5, S14, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use**: Sparse-view reconstruction without regularization, or real-time rendering requirements [S1][S17, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of coordinate-based neural networks for implicit scene representation [S3, unofficial]. The method has become a standard baseline for novel view synthesis and has inspired numerous variants and extensions, including hash-based acceleration [S7, unofficial], multi-scale representations [S21], and text-to-3D generation [S2, unofficial].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard paradigm of optimizing a coordinate-based MLP to represent a 3D scene as a continuous volumetric radiance field, optimized via stochastic gradient descent on photometric loss [S5, unofficial]. The method has since become the foundation for numerous extensions in 3D vision, including differentiable rendering, inverse rendering, and 3D generation [S24].
 
 ## Sources
